  <title>Netemu</title>
  <p>A Netemu is a wire that emulates a network link. The packets between its two switches wait in a buffer, which drains at the bandwidth of the link; they take its delay, and some are lost. The link has states, each with its own values, and it moves between them by chance, as a link that degrades and recovers: the states and the transitions between them are a <link xref="netemu#markov">Markov chain</link>. It runs <cmd>vde-netemu</cmd>, which Debian and Ubuntu don't ship; see <link xref="programs"/>.</p>
  <figure>
    <title>The settings of a Netemu: its ends and its states</title>
    <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/netemu-settings.png">
      <p>The settings of ne1: Left end, sw1, and Right end, sw2; under States of the Markov chain, good, 125000 bytes/s · 10 ms · 0 % lost, selected; bad, 12500 bytes/s · 200 ms · 5 % lost; and down, 125000 bytes/s · 0 ms · 100 % lost; then the buttons + and − and the line The emulator starts in the first state.</p>
    </media>
  </figure>
  <section id="ends">
    <title>Ends</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Left end</gui>, <gui>Right end</gui></title>
        <p>The switches at the two ends, as those of a wire: see <link xref="wires#ends"/>. The values go from left to right, from the left end to the right one, and from right to left. The socket card of a virtual machine can only be the right end.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="states">
    <title>States of the Markov chain</title>
    <p>The list has the states of the link, each with its name and its values, as <code>125000 bytes/s · 0 ms · 0 % lost</code>. The link starts in the first state. Select a state to see its values, and to change them, under <gui>Values of the selected state</gui>.</p>
    <p>The <gui>+</gui> button under the list adds a state after the selected one, named <sys>state 2</sys> or the next free number, with the default values and no chance of moving to it. The <gui>−</gui> button removes the selected state, unless it is the only one.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="values">
    <title>Values of the selected state</title>
    <figure>
      <title>The values of the state good</title>
      <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/netemu-values.png">
        <p>Under Values of the selected state, those of good: Name, good; Bandwidth, 125000; Delay, 10; Buffer, 75000; Loss, 0.00. Each has its number from left to right, →, then its number from right to left, ←, greyed and the same, and the switch Both ways, on.</p>
      </media>
    </figure>
    <p>The values of the selected state:</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Name</gui></title>
        <p>The name of the state, in the list and in the grid of the transitions. Two states can't have the same name.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Bandwidth</gui></title>
        <p>Bytes per second at which the buffer drains, 0 for no limit; 125000 by default, a megabit per second.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Delay</gui></title>
        <p>The delay, in milliseconds, that a packet takes one way, on top of its time in the buffer; 0 by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Buffer</gui></title>
        <p>Bytes that the buffer holds, 0 for no limit; 75000 by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Loss</gui></title>
        <p>The percentage of packets lost, from 0 to 100, as 0.1 for one in a thousand; 0 by default.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>Each value has two numbers, <gui>→</gui> from left to right and <gui>←</gui> from right to left, and the switch <gui>Both ways</gui>. While it is on, the default, the value is the same both ways, the one of <gui>→</gui>, and <gui>←</gui> is greyed. Turn it off for a link that differs each way, as an ADSL line.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="transitions">
    <title>Transitions of the Markov chain</title>
    <figure>
      <title>The transitions between good, bad and down</title>
      <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/netemu-transitions.png">
        <p>Under Transitions of the Markov chain: Period, 1000; then Chances, a grid from a row to a column, with good, bad and down. From good, 5.00 to bad and 1.00 to down, and 94 % stays; from bad, 50.00 to good and 5.00 to down, and 45 % stays; from down, 20.00 to good and 0.00 to bad, and 80 % stays.</p>
      </media>
    </figure>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Period</gui></title>
        <p>How often, in milliseconds, the link may change state; 100 by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Chances</gui></title>
        <p>A grid with a row and a column for each state. The number in the row of a state and the column of another is the chance, in %, of moving from the first to the second at each period. What a state doesn't give to the others it keeps: its own cell, greyed, says how much, as <code>90 % stays</code>. The chances of a row add up to 100 % at most; more is an error, which keeps <gui>OK</gui> greyed.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>With a single state, the link keeps its values.</p>
    <note>
      <p>The three states of the pictures, and a period of 1000 ms: <sys>good</sys>; <sys>bad</sys>, slow, late and lossy; and <sys>down</sys>, which loses every packet. From <sys>good</sys>, 5 % to <sys>bad</sys> and 1 % to <sys>down</sys>; from <sys>bad</sys>, 50 % to <sys>good</sys> and 5 % to <sys>down</sys>; from <sys>down</sys>, 20 % to <sys>good</sys>. Each second, a good link turns bad once in twenty times and goes down once in a hundred; a bad one recovers half of the times; and a link that is down comes back after 5 seconds on average.</p>
    </note>
  </section>
  <section id="markov">
    <title>Markov chains</title>
    <p>A Markov chain is a system that is always in one of a few states, and moves between them by chance, a step at a time. At each step the chance of each move depends on the state it is in, and on nothing else: not on the states before, nor on how long it has been in this one. The chain has no memory. Its chances are a grid, the transition matrix, with a row for each state it moves from and a column for each state it moves to; since at each step it moves or stays, a row adds up to 100 %.</p>
    <p>Markov chains model the weather, a queue at a counter, the letters of a text. For a network link, the classic one is that of Gilbert and Elliott, from the 1960s: a good state, which loses few packets, and a bad one, which loses many, so that the losses come in bursts, as on a wireless link. In a Netemu each state has all the values of the link, and a step comes at each <gui>Period</gui>.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="choosing">
    <title>Choosing the chances</title>
    <p>Since the chain has no memory, the link leaves a state as a die shows a six: at each period with the same chance, however long it has waited. A state lasts on average the period divided by its chance of leaving, but most often a single period, and now and then many. A state that keeps 80 % leaves with 20 %, after 5 periods on average: one stay in five lasts a single period, and one in ten more than ten. When it leaves, the link goes to the other states in proportion to their chances: from <sys>bad</sys>, 50 % to <sys>good</sys> and 5 % to <sys>down</sys>, so ten times in eleven to <sys>good</sys>.</p>
    <p>With the three states of the pictures and a period of 1000 ms:</p>
