        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <p><sys>down</sys></p>
          </td>
          <td>
            <p>80 %</p>
          </td>
          <td>
            <p>5 s</p>
          </td>
          <td>
            <p>6 %</p>
          </td>
          <td>
            <p>100 %</p>
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The share of the time is where the link is in the long run, whatever its first state, and it comes from the whole grid, not from a row alone. This link loses 6.6 % of its packets on average, almost all of them while down, but never 6.6 % at a time: none for seconds, then all of them for seconds. A Netemu with a single state and a loss of 6.6 % has the same average, with the losses spread evenly; the traffic tells them apart (see <link xref="netemu#traffic"/>).</p>
    <p>The chances are for each period: with the same chances, a period half as long halves every stay. Divide the period and the chances by the same number to change state at finer times and keep the same stays: 500 ms, with 2.5 % from <sys>good</sys> to <sys>bad</sys> and 0.5 % to <sys>down</sys>, keeps the 16.7 s of <sys>good</sys>. A state never lasts less than a period.</p>
    <p>A state whose column has no chance is never reached, unless it is the first, which the link starts in and never comes back to once it leaves. A new state, which <gui>+</gui> adds with no chances, is such a state until you give it some. A state whose row has no chance keeps 100 %: once there, the link never leaves, until the Netemu starts again. <sys>down</sys> would be one without its 20 % to <sys>good</sys>: the link of the pictures would go down after 75 s on average, and stay down. So would a group of states that the others lead to, and that leads back to none of them.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="traffic">
    <title>What the traffic sees</title>
    <p>The chain is one for the link, not one for each way: when the state changes, both ways change at once, and so do the answers, as the acknowledgments of TCP.</p>
    <p>A packet takes the values of the state at the moments it crosses the link: when it enters, whether it is lost and whether the buffer has room for it; when its turn in the buffer comes, the bandwidth; when it leaves the buffer, the delay. On its way it keeps what it was given, so a change of state reaches the other end late, by the time in the buffer and the delay. When the link of the pictures goes from <sys>bad</sys> to <sys>down</sys>, the packets already in it still arrive, for 200 ms and more; those that enter while it is down are lost, even those that would arrive after it comes back.</p>
    <p>Each packet leaves when its own delay ends. When the delay falls, as from the 200 ms of <sys>bad</sys> to the 10 ms of <sys>good</sys>, the packets that enter after the change overtake those that entered before it, and arrive out of order. When it rises, the other end gets nothing for about the difference, 190 ms.</p>
    <p>A full buffer takes its size divided by the bandwidth to drain: 75000 bytes, the default, take 0.6 s at the 125000 bytes/s of <sys>good</sys>, but 6 s at the 12500 of <sys>bad</sys>. When the link turns bad under a heavy flow, as a download, the buffer fills: the packets wait in it up to 6 s on top of the delay, and once it is full those that come are dropped, many more than the 5 % of the state. A smaller buffer in a slow state keeps the wait short, and drops more.</p>
    <p>The losses come in bursts, which the protocols feel more than as many losses spread evenly. TCP takes a loss for congestion, and slows down; after an outage, as the 5 s of <sys>down</sys>, it waits longer and longer between its tries, so that a connection may come back seconds after the link. What runs on UDP and doesn't resend, as a call or a stream, has gaps instead: it stutters while the link is bad, and stops while it is down.</p>
    <p>Each start draws anew: two runs of the same lab never see the same states at the same times. A short test can miss a rare state: the link of the pictures goes down 45 times an hour, once every 80 s on average, and a test of a minute may never see it.</p>
    <p>To follow the chain of a running Netemu, open its console with <gui>Open Control Monitor</gui>, in the menu of the brick (see <link xref="bricks#process"/>). <cmd>showcurrent</cmd> tells the state the link is in, <cmd>markov-debug 1</cmd> prints each change as it comes, and <cmd>markov-setnode <var>n</var></cmd> moves the link to the state <var>n</var>, counting from 0 in the order of the list; from there it moves on by chance.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="running">
    <title>While it runs</title>
    <p>The states, their values, the period and the chances change at once in a Netemu that runs; the ends when it starts again. Each start begins in the first state.</p>
  </section>
